course: dictionary
lesson: 7878
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. The word rally is a good example. «Before an election, candidates try to get noticed the way a new cereal does: adverts, catchy slogans, posters, signs on lawns and big cheering rallies.» Here the words election, candidates and cheering show that a rally is a big public meeting.
explain: The same word can mean something very different in another sentence. In tennis, a rally is a series of hits back and forth before a point is won. In car racing, a rally is a race on rough roads. Always ask: what is happening around this word? The clues will tell you which meaning fits.
explain: Rally can also be a verb, a word for an action. To rally means to come together to support someone, or to recover strength. When a losing team comes back from behind, we say it rallied. When prices go up again after falling, we say they rally.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: Rally is a noun (a big meeting, a car race, or a series of hits in a game) and a verb (to come together, or to recover).
fact: «Rallies» fire up supporters, meaning they get people excited about a cause.
fact: «Hyperbole» is an exaggeration, a claim that is much bigger than the truth.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «Before an election, candidates try to get noticed... and big cheering rallies.» In this sentence, what is a rally? | *A big public meeting where people gather to show support | A race for cars on rough roads | A series of hits in a game of tennis | A short rest to get your strength back
exercise: In badminton, pupils try to keep a long rally going. What does rally mean here? | A tiring walk around the court | A large crowd cheering in the stands | *A series of hits back and forth before a point is won | A car race on rough roads
exercise: In sport stories, a team that «rallied» came back from behind. What does rallied mean? | Hid in the changing room | *Recovered and came back strong | Sang a song together | Gave up and went home
test: «Real people pile on hyperbole... "a million people at the rally" in a square that holds 5,000 is an exaggeration.» What does this tell you about the claim "a million people"? | Exactly the right number | *It is far bigger than the truth, so it is an exaggeration | It is a fact from a map | A number too small to count
test: Dad says his friends will rally round to help us move house. What does rally round mean? | Count the crowd at a meeting | Run away quickly | Play a point of tennis | *Come together to help
exam: In money news, prices «rally» after falling. What does rally mean here? | Gather a crowd in a square | *Go up again after falling | Score a point in a game | Fall to the ground
